12	// scatter. It is one workgroup because the whole input is `tokens * used`
13	// integers — 5120 at a 512-token ubatch, 40 960 at the 4 k fixture — and 97%
14	// of this block's time is in the two GEMMs behind it.
15	//
16	// **L5a-4 is why the schedule is a list and not a grid.** At a 512-token
17	// ubatch only **274 of 512 experts are touched**, one of them takes **484 of
18	// the 512 tokens** and the other 273 have fewer than 19: a workgroup per
19	// expert has a 26x load imbalance and a grid of (experts x fixed row blocks)
20	// is almost entirely empty. So this kernel emits one record per (expert, row
21	// block) that has rows — `tileCount` of them — and the GEMM dispatches a
22	// static upper bound of `ceil(tokens*used/BM) + nExperts` row blocks, every
23	// one past `tileCount` returning before it reads anything.
24	//
25	// **Each expert's rows are padded up to `pc.gemmN`, and that padding is what
26	// buys the GEMM its epilogue.** A cooperative-matrix store covers sixteen
27	// rows at once and cannot be masked, so a tile whose expert runs out of rows
28	// part-way through would have to stage its accumulators in LDS and copy them
29	// out under a bound — which is 8-16 KB of LDS on a kernel whose occupancy is
30	// already one wave a SIMD. Rounding every expert's range up instead means a
31	// tile's rows are *always* that expert's, the store goes straight out, and
32	// the padding rows are named by a **sentinel** permutation entry pointing at
33	// a token index one past the batch, whose activation row is zero.
